 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1061 23721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2968451-7379-5754-b957-4337d8ab3c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1061 23722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15d98898-5aa7-5f1a-9c11-0b4e8416b8d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to evaluate the line integral with respect to arc length\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_Cf(x,y)\\:\\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y),$ where $C$ is a curve in the $xy$-plane defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta),\\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this line integral, we need to recall the following facts:\n\n- A polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\n- The arc length element $\\textrm d s$ in plane polar coordinates is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formulas into our line integral, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's go through an example in which we apply this result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1065 3987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f970309b-6c25-53f1-a3b5-b094ebe87109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343993 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343993 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343993 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c7eee1f5-4e67-5791-ab19-b18c7b3e839f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344166 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344166 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344166 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbc4b0b3-3161-5573-8ff6-5f7ebe4c7119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344228 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344228 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344228 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e210fd40-0385-5c02-968e-6174892fbc40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344384 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344384 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344384 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3c07b9f-3789-507f-97de-8e306b0fb0a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344400 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344400 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344400 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f5abf403-18d1-5fd6-8efd-732e33e8d35e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186344384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344718 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7df0e8e5-c868-5ece-818b-a9164009ba5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345247 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d3824d0-05f9-53c5-8fe3-dac14910620a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345247 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{3}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5/2}-{0}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
